reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
The deal decides a large share of any single hand. Over 40 hands, skill controls the size of results:
- folding bad hands limits a loss to 1 instead of 2 to 4;
- a re-put with a strong hand extracts 4;
- bluffed PUTs and RE-PUTs win 1 to 2 points without a showdown.
A rough estimate is that chance decides most single hands but well under half of a 40-hand match between unequal players.

What there is to infer and act on:
- the opponent's face-up card, which shows part of their strength;
- the opponent's call pattern across hands, which reveals their bluff frequency;
- the order of play (6x6), which is a mixed-strategy guessing game.

With both players playing well, the result is a close contest decided by many deals and the order guesses, not by one hidden type. A reasoning player clearly beats an always-see or always-fold player.
